---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { meta, releases } from '../data/site';

const key = process.env.SITE_KEY || 'autobot';
---
<Base
  path="/privacy"
  title="Privacy"
  description={
    key === 'invincible'
      ? 'How Invincible keeps data on hardware you own: local-routing policy, redaction before cloud, signed receipts, hash-chained audit and no telemetry.'
      : 'The Autobot privacy model: offline by default, a visible kill switch, redaction before cloud, encryption at rest, panic wipe and no telemetry.'
  }
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="page-head__meta">
        <span class="eyebrow">Privacy model</span>
        <span class="pill">Code, not a promise</span>
      </div>
      <h1>
        {key === 'invincible' ? <>Nothing leaves<br /><em>unless you say so.</em></> : key === 'sovware' ? <>Privacy is<br /><em>the product.</em></> : <>Nothing leaves<br /><em>unless you see it.</em></>}
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {key === 'invincible'
          ? 'Invincible is built so the default path is local and every exception is visible. This page describes what the code enforces, where the limits are, and how to check the record yourself.'
          : key === 'sovware'
            ? 'Both products share one stance: local is the default, egress is visible and reversible, and the record belongs to the person who made the request. Below: how each one enforces it — and what this website does, too.'
            : 'Most “private AI” means a policy page. Autobot means a kill switch before DNS, a receipt on every answer, redaction before the cloud, and an encrypted database with a panic wipe. This page is the detail.'}
      </p>
      <div class="page-head__aside">
        <div>No telemetry</div>
        <div>No accounts</div>
        <div>No third-party requests</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>


  <div class="shell">
    <dl class="trustbar">
      <div><dt>Default</dt><dd><span class="dot"></span>Local / offline</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Egress</dt><dd>Visible &amp; switchable</dd></div>
      <div><dt>At rest</dt><dd>Encrypted</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Telemetry</dt><dd>None</dd></div>
      <div><dt>This site</dt><dd>No cookies, no analytics</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
  {key === 'sovware' ? (
    <div class="section">
      <div class="shell">
        <div class="products">
          <a class="product" href="https://autobot.sh/privacy">
            <div class="product__top"><span class="pill pill--red">On the phone</span><span class="product__index">01</span></div>
            <div>
              <h2 class="product__bm">Auto<em>bot</em></h2>
              <p>Offline by default. A kill switch before DNS. Redaction, SQLCipher, a panic wipe, and a receipt on every answer.</p>
            </div>
            <span class="product__go">Read Autobot’s model <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
          </a>
          <a class="product" href="https://invincible.sh/privacy">
            <div class="product__top"><span class="pill pill--red">On your node</span><span class="product__index">02</span></div>
            <div>
              <h2 class="product__bm">Invin<em>cible</em></h2>
              <p>Local routing by policy, signed receipts, a hash-chained audit log, encrypted keys and no telemetry.</p>
            </div>
            <span class="product__go">Read the node’s model <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
          </a>
        </div>

        <div class="article mt-4">
          <div class="article__toc">
            <h2>This site</h2>
            <a href="#site">No tracking</a>
            <a href="#fonts">Fonts</a>
            <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
          </div>
          <div class="article__body">
            <section id="site">
              <h2>This website.</h2>
              <p>No analytics, no cookies, no fingerprinting, no session recording, no third-party scripts. Fonts are self-hosted. There is no runtime call to GitHub either: release data is baked at build time. The only external links are the ones you click.</p>
              <p>The site is served as static files with a Content-Security-Policy that permits no third-party origins, HSTS, and no server-side application. That is also why it stays up: there is nothing to keep alive.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="fonts">
              <h2>Type.</h2>
              <p>Antonio and JetBrains Mono are self-hosted WOFF2 files from this origin. No request goes to Google Fonts, so reading this page does not tell a third party that you did.</p>
            </section>
            <section id="limits">
              <h2>The limits.</h2>
              <p>Privacy engineering is not magic. A compromised phone or server is compromised; a model provider still sees what you send after redaction; a screenshot is a screenshot. The claim here is narrower and more useful: <b>the software does not create data flows you did not ask for</b>, and it leaves a record when one exists.</p>
            </section>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : key === 'invincible' ? (
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="article">
        <aside class="article__toc">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <a href="#default">Local by default</a>
          <a href="#egress">Network &amp; egress</a>
          <a href="#redaction">Redaction</a>
          <a href="#receipts">Receipts</a>
          <a href="#audit">Audit log</a>
          <a href="#keys">Keys</a>
          <a href="#site">This site</a>
          <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
        </aside>
        <div class="article__body">
          <section id="default">
            <h2>Local is the default.</h2>
            <p>Every request is evaluated by the routing rules in <b>spec/routing.md</b>, pinned by 51 conformance vectors shared with Autobot. If the rules do not explicitly allow a remote tier, the request runs on a local engine. A request marked <code>invincible-private</code> is never eligible for the cloud.</p>
            <p>Cloud backends are optional and off unless configured. The node does not need a frontier provider to answer; it needs one only if your rules choose one.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="egress">
            <h2>Network and egress.</h2>
            <p>The machine has one network mode — <b>offline</b>, <b>direct</b>, <b>Tor</b> or <b>SOCKS5</b> — and cloud backends and the Telegram bot follow it. With <code>egress = "never"</code>, nothing leaves; with <code>ask</code>, a request that would leave answers 409 until it is repeated with confirmation; with <code>auto</code>, the rules decide.</p>
            <p>Blocked requests answer 403, 404 or 503 with the reason. The node listens on loopback and paired interfaces only; nothing is exposed to the open internet by default.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="redaction">
            <h2>Redaction before the wire.</h2>
            <p>Before a request goes to a cloud backend, names, emails, phone numbers, street addresses, card numbers, IBANs and account numbers become placeholders (<code>[PERSON_1]</code>, <code>[EMAIL_1]</code>). Later mentions of a known value are hidden too. The answer gets the real values back on the node, streaming included.</p>
            <p>The <code>invincible-redacted</code> response header counts what was hidden. The same rules run on the phone; both sides are checked against the same redaction vectors.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="receipts">
            <h2>Signed receipts.</h2>
            <p>Every completion answers with an <code>invincible-request-id</code>. Once complete, <code>GET /v1/receipts/&lt;id&gt;</code> returns a receipt signed with the node’s Ed25519 key. It records the routing decision, tokens, what was redacted, and every attempt to reach a host off the node, with bytes sent and received.</p>
            <p>An empty <code>egress</code> list is a signed statement that nothing left the node. Receipts never contain prompt or answer text. Paired devices pin the node’s key at pairing and verify the signature themselves.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="audit">
            <h2>A record that notices edits.</h2>
            <p>Every request is also written to <code>~/.invincible/audit.jsonl</code>: the signed receipt, when, and who asked. Each entry carries the hash of the one before it, so removing or editing an entry breaks the chain.</p>
            <Terminal
              title="check the record"
              command="invincible audit --verify"
              lines={[
                { kind: 'ok', text: '# chain intact · every receipt signature valid' },
                { kind: 'cmd', text: 'invincible audit --since 7d --summary' },
                { kind: 'dim', text: '# totals per model, and every host anything was sent to' },
              ]}
              copyText="invincible audit --verify"
            />
          </section>

          <section id="keys">
            <h2>Keys and data at rest.</h2>
            <p>Node data is encrypted at rest, with keys in the OS keychain or a TPM / Secure Enclave where present. Cloud API keys live in files or environment variables, never in the config, and are never logged. Pairing keys are pinned on both sides and can be revoked from either side.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="site">
            <h2>This website.</h2>
            <p>No analytics, cookies, fingerprinting or third-party scripts. Fonts are self-hosted; release data is baked at build time; the install script is mirrored and its hash published. The site is static, with a CSP that permits no third-party origins.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="limits">
            <h2>The limits, stated plainly.</h2>
            <p>Invincible is <b>early build</b>. The core paths are implemented and tested, but the independent security review is still ahead. Encryption at rest and platform key stores are designed, not independently audited. Run it on a machine you control, keep it patched, and do not treat v0.x as hardened infrastructure.</p>
            <p>A cloud provider still sees the prompt after redaction. Redaction reduces exposure; it does not make a remote model local. The point of the project is that you can choose not to use one.</p>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : (
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="article">
        <aside class="article__toc">
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <a href="#short">The short version</a>
        <a href="#network">Network</a>
        <a href="#redaction">Redaction</a>
        <a href="#cascade">Phone first</a>
        <a href="#rest">At rest</a>
        <a href="#export">Export</a>
        <a href="#permissions">Permissions</a>
        <a href="#site">This site</a>
        <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
      </aside>
      <div class="article__body">
        <section id="short">
          <h2>The short version.</h2>
          <p>Autobot starts with the network <b>off</b>. Chat, agents, images, voice and recipes run on the phone. When you choose a cloud provider, the request passes the kill switch, the route, redaction and the audit log, and the answer carries a receipt saying what happened.</p>
          <p>There is no telemetry, no account, no analytics SDK and no device identifier. The User-Agent is the constant <code>Autobot</code>. Backups and device transfer are disabled.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="network">
          <h2>A network you can see.</h2>
          <p>The modes are <b>Offline</b>, <b>Direct</b>, <b>Tor</b> (Orbot SOCKS <code>127.0.0.1:9050</code>, remote DNS) and <b>SOCKS5</b>. Each provider can override the route, but a <code>Direct</code> override can never downgrade a global Tor or SOCKS5 mode.</p>
          <p>A shared <code>KillSwitch</code> rejects every request before DNS or socket I/O. Loopback servers such as a local Ollama can still be reached; that can be switched off too. The Privacy Center shows a live, in-memory audit of every attempt — time, method, host, path, sizes, tag, blocked or not. Headers, query strings and bodies are never recorded.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>No HTTP cache, no cookies, no automatic redirect following.</li>
            <li>Model downloads follow redirects hop by hop; every hop passes the kill switch and audit log.</li>
            <li>Hub tokens are sent only to their own host, never to a CDN redirect.</li>
            <li>HTTPS uses system CAs only. Cleartext is limited to <code>localhost</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code> and <code>10.0.2.2</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="redaction">
          <h2>Redaction before cloud.</h2>
          <p>On by default. Names, emails, phone numbers, addresses, card, IBAN and account numbers, and memory facts become placeholders like <code>[PERSON_1]</code> before a cloud provider sees the request. The answer gets the real values back on the phone. Each chat can switch it off; the receipt counts what was hidden.</p>
          <p>The rules are shared Kotlin (<code>dev.wckdboy.autobot.shared.redaction</code>) and checked against the node’s vectors, so the phone and an Invincible node hide the same things.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="cascade">
          <h2>Phone first, when you want it.</h2>
          <p>Off by default. With it on, a plain chat on a cloud model is answered by the on-device model first. A small judge — rules plus one short self-check on the same model — decides whether the answer is good enough.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Ask</b> offers the cloud model after a weak local answer.</li>
            <li><b>Auto</b> sends a weak answer’s prompt on its own, unless it looks personal.</li>
            <li><b>Never</b> keeps everything on the phone.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Both answers stay on screen, the cloud request goes through redaction, and the receipt says why. A paired node can sit in the middle: phone → node → cloud, with “never” meaning “never beyond hardware I own”.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="rest">
          <h2>Encrypted at rest.</h2>
          <p>Conversations live in Room over <b>SQLCipher</b>. A random 256-bit database key is wrapped by an AES-256-GCM Android Keystore key, StrongBox when available. API keys are encrypted the same way, never logged, and their <code>toString()</code> is redacted. On iOS: data protection (<code>NSFileProtectionComplete</code>), Keychain plus Secure Enclave wrapping, and a privacy cover in the app switcher.</p>
          <p><b>Incognito</b> chats are held in memory only and never touch the database. <b>Panic wipe</b> — type <code>WIPE</code> — destroys Keystore keys, wrapped keys, databases, settings, secrets and caches, then kills the process. A duress PIN and wipe-after-failed-unlocks are available for worse days.</p>
          <p><code>FLAG_SECURE</code> is set and recents screenshots are disabled. An optional app lock uses biometrics or the device credential, with an auto-lock timeout. The keyboard is asked not to learn from what you type.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="export">
          <h2>An exit you control.</h2>
          <p>Settings → Export &amp; import writes one <code>.autobot</code> file sealed with your passphrase: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 at 600k iterations, AES-256-GCM in 1 MiB chunks, with the header, chunk index and final flag authenticated so tampering, reordering and truncation are rejected.</p>
          <p>API keys are never exported. An import never switches the app online or changes the app lock. The other platform’s app opens the same file — the format is shared code, tested on both sides.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="permissions">
          <h2>Minimal permissions.</h2>
          <p>Android asks for <code>INTERNET</code> and <code>USE_BIOMETRIC</code>, plus <code>RUN_USER_INITIATED_JOBS</code>, <code>ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE</code> and <code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code> so a model download can keep running as a user-initiated data transfer job with progress. The notification permission is requested only when the first download starts.</p>
          <p><code>RECORD_AUDIO</code> is requested only when you first tap the microphone. Audio is transcribed on the phone and never stored or sent anywhere. Diagnostics show the device, accelerators and benchmarks — never prompts, file paths or identifiers — and nothing is sent unless you copy it.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="site">
          <h2>This website.</h2>
          <p>No analytics, cookies, fingerprinting, session recording or third-party scripts. Fonts are self-hosted. Release data is baked at build time, not fetched from your browser. The only external requests are the ones you click. The site is static files with a CSP that permits no third-party origins and HSTS on the wire.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="limits">
          <h2>What this does not claim.</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>A compromised phone is compromised. Encryption at rest protects data when the device is off or locked, not a live attacker with your unlocked phone.</li>
            <li>A cloud provider still sees the prompt after redaction. Redaction reduces exposure; it does not make a remote model local. The answer is to use one less.</li>
            <li>The Snapdragon accelerator paths still need verification on the 8 Elite Gen 5 hardware they were built for. The diagnostics report is how that closes.</li>
            <li>The Android APK is signed and hashes are published, but the app has not had an independent security audit.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="hero__actions">
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/verify">Verify a download</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={meta.repo} rel="noopener">Read the code <span class="btn__arrow">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  )}
</Base>
